  "notes": "Cresud SACIF y A (NASDAQ: CRESY; BCBA: CRES) \u2014 leading Argentine agricultural company holding ~590,000 hectares (~1.46M acres) of productive farmland across Argentina, Bolivia, Paraguay, and (via 33% stake in BrasilAgro / AGRO3.SA) Brazil. Also owns ~53.7% of IRSA, the largest Argentine real-estate company (shopping centers, offices). Reports in inflation-adjusted ARS under IAS 29 (Argentina has been hyperinflationary since July 2018); files 20-F with SEC. Fiscal year ends June 30. Five operating segments: Agricultural Business (own farms \u2014 crops, beef, dairy, sugarcane), Real Estate Argentina (IRSA), Real Estate International, BrasilAgro (consolidated subsidiary), and Other. CIK 1034957. USD figures here are derived from official ARS press releases / annual reports translated at the closing ARS/USD rate at the relevant period end (Cresud's own 20-F translation methodology). FY2018 and prior use nominal ARS divided by closing FX. FY2019+ use IAS-29-restated ARS divided by closing FX which is approximate (the cleanest official source is the 20-F's Selected Financial Data table). Argentine peso devaluation distorts year-over-year comparability \u2014 USD figures are the cleanest cut. Net debt is consolidated (includes IRSA + BrasilAgro debt, much of which is non-recourse to Cresud parent). Sources: 20-F filings via SEC EDGAR, FY18 Annual Report (Cresud IR site), FY19/FY20/FY21/FY22/FY23/FY24 earnings release press releases via PRNewswire.",
